'''Sanapana''' ({{lang|spn|sanapana payvoma}}<ref name=":1">{{Cite journal |last=Unruh |first=Ernesto |last2=Kalisch |first2=Hannes |date=2003 |title=Enlhet-Enenlhet. Una familia lingüística chaqueña. |url=http://www.enlhet.org/pdf/nne28-enlhet-enenlhet.pdf |journal=Thule: Rivista italiana di studi americanistici |volume=14/15 |pages=207–231}}</ref>) is a [[Mascoian languages|Mascoian language]] of the Paraguayan [[Gran Chaco people|Chaco]], spoken by the [[Sanapaná people]]. The language is not critically endangered, as children are still learning it.

== Status ==
Sanapana is spoken by around half of all Sanapana people, who call themselves {{lang|spn|nenlhet}},<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://pueblosoriginarios.com/sur/chaco/sanapana/sanapana.html|title=Sanapaná|website=pueblosoriginarios.com|accessdate=5 April 2021}}</ref><ref name=":1" /> and is still being transmitted to children in the two largest communities, La Esperanza and Anaconda, who grow up monolingual in Sanapana before they go to school, where they learn Spanish. The language is also reported to be in everyday use, including in religious contexts.<ref name=":0" />{{Reference page|page=5}}

== Classification ==
Sanapana is classified as a member of the [[Mascoian languages|Mascoian (Enlhet-Enenlhet) languages]], distributed in the Gran Chaco region.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Lengua Sanapaná |url=http://www.proel.org/index.php?pagina=mundo/amerindia/macropanoan/maskoian/sanapana |access-date=2024-09-01 |website=Promotora Española de Lingüística}}</ref><ref name=":1" /> They are considered to have evolved from a [[dialect continuum]].<ref name=":0" />{{Reference page|page=4}}

